x86/mm: free the gaps of the kernel image with no poison

free_init_pages() wrote POISON_FREE_INITMEM to each page that it
freed: the init sections, the SMP alternatives, and the gaps around
rodata. The pages of the gaps were not written before, and in a
guest the first write to a page is a fault in the host, so the
poison of 2.3 MiB took about 1 ms. The pages now go to the page
allocator as they are. A page from the allocator has no defined
contents, so the users of the pages see no change.

Measured on KVM, 1 CPU, 64 MB, root on dm-verity, two runs of 8 boots:
the mount of the root to the start of init was 1.0 ms and 1.8 ms
(mean) before, and 0.27 ms and 0.24 ms after.

--- a/arch/x86/mm/init.c
+++ b/arch/x86/mm/init.c
@@ -934,8 +934,12 @@
 		set_memory_nx(begin, (end - begin) >> PAGE_SHIFT);
 		set_memory_rw(begin, (end - begin) >> PAGE_SHIFT);
 
-		free_reserved_area((void *)begin, (void *)end,
-				   POISON_FREE_INITMEM, what);
+		/*
+		 * No poison: the memset() of the poison is the first write
+		 * to the pages of the gaps, and in a guest each first write
+		 * to a page is a fault in the host.
+		 */
+		free_reserved_area((void *)begin, (void *)end, -1, what);
 	}
 }
 
